  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nNear term through Thursday...Main concern are chances for\nscattered showers this afternoon. Already added 20-30% chances \nfor scattered showers this afternoon especially over east- \ncentral WI. Peak timing will be from 2p-5p. Forcing will be \nprovided by shortwave over northern Iowa and weak 850-600mb \nfrontogenesis enhanced by right entrance of upper jet. Thunder\npotential low even as shower coverage will be maximized during \npeak heating as MUCAPE will be well under 500 J/kg being held in \ncheck by current cloud cover. Limited sunshine will lead to highs\nthis afternoon staying in the 70s for most areas.\n\nRisk for scattered showers ends early this evening. Once clouds\nclear out, temps cooling into the upper 50s and light winds could\nlead to areas of fog especially northeast and east-central WI. MOS\nguidance suggests that the fog could become locally dense. After\nthe fog diminishes early Thursday morning, expect skies to be mostly\nsunny as high pressure will be overhead. Will see scattered fair wx\ncu through the afternoon. Highs will be warmer than today, with\nmany locations reaching the lower 80s. \n\nShower and thunderstorm chances beginning Friday...Weak front\ndrops across WI Friday afternoon through late Friday night,\ninteracting with ribbon of higher instability (MLCAPEs 1000+ \nJ/kg) will allow showers and scattered storms to blossom over \nnorthern WI. Strongest shortwave and large scale lift stays north \nof Lake Superior late Friday, so expect greatest coverage of\nshowers to stay over northern WI into Upper Michigan through\nFriday evening. This is also where smaller chances of any severe\nstorms will be near shear over 30 kts. Smaller chances of showers\nand storms will spread over the rest of the area Friday evening as\nthe front shifts east/southeast. \n\nFor the weekend and into early next week, western Great Lakes\nregion remains on edge of western ridge/heat dome. Thus we will \nstay on the gradient of steeper mid-level lapse rates over the \ncentral Plains. Better chances of more organized convection will \nremain south and west, but seasonably stronger northwest flow and \nany shortwaves within the flow could kick off at least a couple \nrounds of showers and storms this weekend through Monday. Overall\nthough given lack of stronger shortwaves and/or abundant instability\npops remain pretty low throughout. Based on LREF which is blend of\nensembles, best signal for rain (probabilities of 0.05 inch or \nmore) would be late Saturday night, then again Sunday night \nthrough Monday.\n\nIn terms of severe and heavy rain potential, machine learning \nguidance has increased signal for possible severe nearby both \nSaturday and Sunday. Greater risks are still southwest of Wisconsin\nthough. ECMWF EFI points to Sunday as the greater risk day, but \neven then the stronger signal remains over northern IL and southern\nWI. Will continue to monitor for any severe weather potential. \nSimilar pattern to the risk for heavy rain, with parts of central \nWI placed in a marginal risk (1 of 5) for Saturday/Saturday night \nand Sunday/Sunday night. Frontal boundary over the area early next\nweek gradually will sag south leading to lower chances for rain \nby Tuesday/Wednesday. \n\nTemperatures/smoke...Temperatures will be near normal into next\nweek with highs in the lower to middle 80s. Humidity levels will \nbe comfortable, with dewpoints reading mainly in the upper 50s to \nlow 60s. Elevated smoke layer departs by this evening. Another\npush of elevated smoke returns on Friday ahead of the approaching\nfront. No significant impacts to visibility or air quality are \nexpected.",
